WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.842
Constraints turn important business rules into rules the database can enforce.

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:07.722
NOT NULL says a value is required.

00:00:07.722 --> 00:00:11.192
DEFAULT supplies a value when one is omitted.

00:00:11.192 --> 00:00:15.532
UNIQUE protects candidate keys or other non-duplicate rules.

00:00:15.532 --> 00:00:18.452
CHECK evaluates a Boolean condition.

00:00:18.452 --> 00:00:21.272
PRIMARY KEY defines row identity.

00:00:21.272 --> 00:00:24.787
FOREIGN KEY protects relationships to parent rows.

00:00:24.787 --> 00:00:31.472
One beginner detail is especially important: CHECK does not automatically make a column required.

00:00:31.472 --> 00:00:38.577
For example, a check that quantity is greater than zero only evaluates the quantity when a value is

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:43.217
present. If quantity must always be present, combine the check with NOT NULL.

00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:50.917
The database should enforce important rules whenever possible instead of relying only on application code or

00:00:50.917 --> 00:00:57.112
user memory. Connect this idea to a clinic system separating patients, encounters, clinicians, and billing.

00:00:57.112 --> 00:01:02.607
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:09.972
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:09.972 --> 00:01:15.452
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:15.452 --> 00:01:21.120
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
